# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BOOK_INTROS_OT = [
    ("Genesis", "Beginnings",
     "Author & Date: Traditionally Moses, compiling and writing during Israel's wilderness years (events span roughly 2000+ years, from creation to c. 1800s BC).",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israelites freshly delivered from Egypt, needing to know who they were, where they came from, and what God had promised their ancestors before entering Canaan.",
     "Historical Setting: Ancient Near Eastern cultures had their own creation and flood accounts (e.g., Enuma Elish, the Gilgamesh flood story); Genesis both echoes the ancient setting and directly confronts its polytheism with the claim of one sovereign Creator.",
     "Purpose: To establish God as Creator, explain sin's entry into the world, and trace His covenant promise through Abraham's family toward eventual blessing for every nation."),
    ("Exodus", "Redemption and Covenant",
     "Author & Date: Moses, narrating events during Israel's wilderness years after leaving Egypt (traditional dating c. 1440s BC).",
     "Audience & Occasion: The newly freed nation of Israel, needing both the story of their deliverance and the covenant law that would define them as God's people.",
     "Historical Setting: Egypt was the regional superpower of the day; each of the ten plagues arguably confronted a specific Egyptian deity, publicly humiliating Pharaoh's claimed divine authority.",
     "Purpose: To show God redeeming His people by grace alone, then forming them into a covenant nation through His law and His presence among them in the Tabernacle."),
    ("Leviticus", "Holiness for a Redeemed People",
     "Author & Date: Moses, given at Mount Sinai shortly after the events of Exodus.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israel, freshly redeemed from Egypt, needing instruction on how a perfectly holy God could safely dwell among a sinful people.",
     "Historical Setting: Sacrificial systems existed broadly across the Ancient Near East, but Israel's law uniquely joined ethical holiness with ritual worship, grounded in relationship with a personal covenant God rather than capricious deities.",
     "Purpose: To teach Israel practical holiness ('Be holy, because I am holy') and to establish the sacrificial system that anticipates the ultimate atonement accomplished in Christ."),
    ("Numbers", "Wandering and Waiting",
     "Author & Date: Moses, covering roughly 39 years of wilderness wandering after Sinai.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israel, twice numbered by census (hence the book's title) -- once the generation that failed to trust God, and later the new generation preparing to enter the land.",
     "Historical Setting: The narrative moves through real Sinai and Negev wilderness geography, with administrative census and tribal-list formats typical of the Ancient Near East.",
     "Purpose: To show the tragic cost of persistent unbelief and grumbling, while displaying God's ongoing faithfulness to preserve a new generation to inherit His promise."),
    ("Deuteronomy", "Covenant Renewed",
     "Author & Date: Moses' final addresses to Israel on the plains of Moab, shortly before his death.",
     "Audience & Occasion: The new generation about to cross into Canaan, needing the covenant restated and applied freshly to their coming life in the land.",
     "Historical Setting: The book's structure mirrors known Ancient Near Eastern suzerain-vassal treaties -- historical prologue, covenant stipulations, blessings and curses, witnesses -- a covenant renewal document in a form its first hearers would immediately recognize.",
     "Purpose: To call Israel to wholehearted love and obedience toward God as the condition for enjoying the covenant land's blessings."),
    ("Joshua", "Claiming the Promise",
     "Author & Date: Traditionally associated with Joshua (with some later editorial additions, such as the account of his death), covering Israel's conquest and division of Canaan.",
     "Historical Setting: Late Bronze Age Canaan was a patchwork of fortified city-states; the conquest narratives reflect authentic ancient siege warfare and treaty customs, such as the Gibeonites' deceptive peace treaty.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israel entering the land God had promised for centuries, needing courage and singular devotion to God amid surrounding pagan influence.",
     "Purpose: To show God fulfilling His covenant promise of land while calling Israel to wholehearted obedience and exclusive worship of Yahweh alone."),
    ("Judges", "The Danger of Doing What's Right in Your Own Eyes",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, likely compiled during the early monarchy, covering roughly two centuries of a repeating moral and political cycle.",
     "Historical Setting: A loosely confederated, tribal Israel without centralized leadership faced ongoing threats from Canaanite, Ammonite, Midianite, and Philistine peoples.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Later Israelites needing to understand how covenant unfaithfulness produced generations of chaos -- and implicitly, why godly kingship was needed.",
     "Purpose: To show the disastrous spiritual and social consequences of Israel repeatedly abandoning covenant faithfulness for the surrounding culture's gods."),
    ("Ruth", "Quiet Faithfulness, Providential Kindness",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, set 'in the days when the judges ruled,' though likely written or compiled later (the closing genealogy points toward David's reign).",
     "Historical Setting: A story of famine, migration to Moab, and eventual return to Bethlehem set against the broader social chaos of the Judges period.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israelites needing a picture of quiet, ordinary covenant faithfulness (hesed) amid a disordered era, and the origin story of David's own great-grandmother.",
     "Purpose: To display God's providence working through loyal, humble faithfulness, while tracing the lineage that leads to King David -- and ultimately to Christ."),
    ("1 Samuel", "From Judges to Kings",
     "Author & Date: Unknown (Jewish tradition credits Samuel, Gad, and Nathan), covering the transition from the judges' era to the united monarchy.",
     "Historical Setting: The Philistines' iron-age military technology posed a major threat, prompting Israel's demand for a king 'like the other nations.'",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israel navigating Samuel's prophetic leadership, Saul's tragic decline as king, and David's rise despite Saul's relentless persecution.",
     "Purpose: To contrast human kingship gone wrong (Saul) with God's chosen king after His own heart (David), showing God's sovereign hand directing history throughout."),
    ("2 Samuel", "David's Throne, David's Fall",
     "Author & Date: Continuation of 1 Samuel, covering the reign of King David.",
     "Historical Setting: David's military successes and political unification made Israel a regional power for the first time in its history.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israel reflecting on both the glory of the Davidic covenant (2 Samuel 7) and the devastating personal and family consequences of David's affair with Bathsheba.",
     "Purpose: To establish the Davidic covenant as the foundation for all future messianic hope, while honestly recording even Israel's greatest king's serious moral failure and its costs."),
    ("1 Kings", "Wisdom, Wealth, and a Divided Kingdom",
     "Author & Date: Unknown (Jewish tradition suggests Jeremiah), covering Solomon's reign and the kingdom's split into Israel (north) and Judah (south).",
     "Historical Setting: Solomon's era saw unprecedented wealth and international relations (such as the visit of the Queen of Sheba); after the kingdom's division, both halves navigated shifting regional alliances.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Later Israelites needing to understand how the kingdom fractured and why so many of its kings are evaluated so harshly.",
     "Purpose: To evaluate each king by covenant faithfulness, showing that even Solomon's legendary wisdom could not prevent decline rooted in disobedience."),
    ("2 Kings", "The Fall of Both Kingdoms",
     "Author & Date: Continuation of 1 Kings, tracing the decline and eventual exile of both Israel (to Assyria, 722 BC) and Judah (to Babylon, 586 BC).",
     "Historical Setting: The rise of the Assyrian and then Babylonian empires directly shaped this history, a sequence extensively corroborated by extrabiblical Assyrian and Babylonian records.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Exiled or soon-to-be-exiled Israelites, needing to understand that the disaster of exile was neither random nor a sign God had lost -- it was covenant judgment long foretold.",
     "Purpose: To show that persistent covenant unfaithfulness, especially idolatry, brought exactly the covenant curses Deuteronomy had warned of centuries earlier."),
    ("1 Chronicles", "Remembering Who You Are",
     "Author & Date: Likely compiled by Ezra or a similar figure in the post-exilic community, retelling history from Adam through David with extensive genealogies.",
     "Historical Setting: Written for the community recently returned from Babylonian exile, needing to reconnect with their national and priestly identity.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Returned exiles rebuilding a fractured community, needing reassurance that their genealogical and covenant identity had not been erased by the exile.",
     "Purpose: To encourage the returned community by emphasizing God's enduring faithfulness to David's line and the centrality of proper worship."),
    ("2 Chronicles", "Worship, Reform, and Hope",
     "Author & Date: Continuation of 1 Chronicles, retelling the monarchy from Solomon through the exile, closing with Cyrus's decree permitting return.",
     "Historical Setting: Same post-exilic audience as 1 Chronicles, needing hope that God's covenant purposes continued despite the catastrophe of exile.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A community rebuilding worship practices, needing models (both good and bad) of kings who did or didn't prioritize the Temple and true worship.",
     "Purpose: To highlight worship reform and God's continuing faithfulness, ending deliberately on the hopeful note of restoration."),
    ("Ezra", "Rebuilding the Temple",
     "Author & Date: Possibly Ezra himself, describing two returns from Babylonian exile -- first under Zerubbabel to rebuild the Temple, then under Ezra to reform spiritual compromise.",
     "Historical Setting: The Persian Empire under Cyrus and his successors permitted subject peoples to return home and rebuild local worship, a policy corroborated by the Cyrus Cylinder.",
     "Audience & Occasion: The newly returned community, needing both practical leadership to rebuild and spiritual reform to address compromising marriages and practices.",
     "Purpose: To show God fulfilling His promise of restoration and to call the returned community to renewed covenant faithfulness."),
    ("Nehemiah", "Rebuilding the Walls",
     "Author & Date: Likely Nehemiah's own memoir, describing his leadership rebuilding Jerusalem's walls amid fierce local opposition.",
     "Historical Setting: Nehemiah served as a high-ranking Persian court official (cupbearer to King Artaxerxes) before receiving permission to return and rebuild.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A vulnerable, exposed Jerusalem community needing both physical security and a renewed commitment to God's covenant.",
     "Purpose: To show that rebuilding physical and spiritual foundations requires both bold, prayerful leadership and humble dependence on God's word."),
    ("Esther", "God's Hidden Hand",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, set in the Persian court during the reign of King Xerxes (Ahasuerus).",
     "Historical Setting: The Persian Empire's vast, multi-ethnic administration and famously irrevocable legal decrees form the backdrop of the story.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Jewish communities scattered across the Persian Empire, needing assurance of God's protective care even far from the land and Temple.",
     "Purpose: Notably never mentioning God's name directly, the book demonstrates His providential care working behind the scenes of ordinary and political events -- 'for such a time as this.'"),
    ("Job", "Faith Without All the Answers",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, possibly one of the oldest books in the Bible, set in the patriarchal era.",
     "Historical Setting: Job lived in the land of Uz as a wealthy pastoral chieftain, entirely outside Israel's later covenant community.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Any reader wrestling with undeserved suffering, presented through an extended poetic dialogue between Job, his friends, and finally God Himself.",
     "Purpose: To challenge simplistic 'suffering always equals punishment' theology and call for trusting God's wisdom and sovereignty even without full explanations."),
    ("Psalms", "The Prayer Book of the Bible",
     "Author & Date: A collection of 150 songs and prayers written across centuries by various authors (David wrote about half, along with Asaph, the sons of Korah, Solomon, Moses, and others).",
     "Historical Setting: Written across Israel's history from the wilderness (Psalm 90, attributed to Moses) through the monarchy and into or after the exile, reflecting an enormous range of life circumstances.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Compiled into five 'books' echoing the Torah's five books, for use in Israel's corporate and personal worship.",
     "Purpose: To give God's people an inspired vocabulary for worship, lament, confession, and praise across every human emotion and season."),
    ("Proverbs", "Wisdom for Everyday Life",
     "Author & Date: Primarily Solomon, with additional collections from unnamed 'wise' sages, Agur, and King Lemuel.",
     "Historical Setting: Wisdom literature was common across the Ancient Near East (Egyptian and Mesopotamian wisdom texts survive), but Proverbs uniquely roots all true wisdom in 'the fear of the LORD.'",
     "Audience & Occasion: Compiled short, memorable sayings meant to train practical, godly discernment in everyday matters -- speech, work, finances, relationships.",
     "Purpose: To shape character and practical wisdom for ordinary daily living, not abstract philosophical speculation."),
    ("Ecclesiastes", "Life 'Under the Sun'",
     "Author & Date: Traditionally Solomon ('the Preacher' or 'Teacher'), likely reflecting late in his life.",
     "Historical Setting: Written from the perspective of someone with unmatched resources and opportunity to test every avenue of human fulfillment -- wealth, pleasure, wisdom, achievement.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Anyone tempted to build life's meaning on achievement, pleasure, or wisdom pursued for its own sake, apart from God.",
     "Purpose: To expose the emptiness ('vapor,' often translated 'vanity') of a life lived for itself, concluding that fearing God and keeping His commands is 'the whole duty of man.'"),
    ("Song of Songs", "The Goodness of Married Love",
     "Author & Date: Traditionally Solomon, an extended poetic celebration of romantic and sexual love between a husband and wife.",
     "Historical Setting: Uses agricultural and pastoral imagery typical of ancient Israelite courtship and wedding poetry.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Married or betrothed couples, and by long-standing Jewish and Christian tradition, a picture by analogy of God's covenant love for His people.",
     "Purpose: To celebrate the goodness of marital love as part of God's good creation, unashamedly and poetically."),
    ("Isaiah", "The Suffering Servant King",
     "Author & Date: The prophet Isaiah, ministering in Judah across several kings' reigns (roughly 740-680 BC).",
     "Historical Setting: Assyria's rise as the regional superpower threatened both Israel and Judah; later chapters address a future Babylonian exile and return, written centuries before either happened.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Judah, tempted to trust political alliances rather than God amid international threat, later needing hope of eventual restoration from exile.",
     "Purpose: To call Judah to trust God rather than foreign alliances, and to unveil the clearest Old Testament portrait of the coming Suffering Servant Messiah (especially chapters 40-55)."),
    ("Jeremiah", "The Weeping Prophet",
     "Author & Date: The prophet Jeremiah, ministering in Judah's final decades before the Babylonian exile (c. 627-586 BC and beyond).",
     "Historical Setting: Judah's kings largely ignored or persecuted Jeremiah as the Babylonian empire under Nebuchadnezzar grew in power, eventually destroying Jerusalem in 586 BC exactly as he had warned.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A stubborn, unrepentant nation refusing to heed decades of warning, and later, a devastated remnant needing future hope.",
     "Purpose: To call a hardened nation to repentance, while also promising a future 'new covenant' written on the heart (31:31-34)."),
    ("Lamentations", "Grief Given Structure",
     "Author & Date: Traditionally Jeremiah, five poetic laments mourning Jerusalem's destruction and the Temple's fall in 586 BC.",
     "Historical Setting: Written in the immediate, raw aftermath of the Babylonian destruction of Jerusalem.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A devastated community needing permission and structure (the Hebrew acrostic form) to express overwhelming grief honestly before God.",
     "Purpose: To model honest, structured lament, culminating in the well-known affirmation that God's 'mercies... are new every morning' even amid devastating loss."),
    ("Ezekiel", "Visions in Exile",
     "Author & Date: The prophet Ezekiel, a priest exiled to Babylon, prophesying both judgment on Jerusalem before its 586 BC fall and hope of future restoration.",
     "Historical Setting: Ezekiel ministered entirely from exile in Babylon among the first wave of deportees, addressing a community wondering if God had abandoned them or the covenant was void.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Exiled Israelites needing both an honest reckoning with why the exile happened and hope for what would come after it.",
     "Purpose: To vindicate God's holiness, explain the exile as deserved judgment, and promise future restoration through a new heart and spirit."),
    ("Daniel", "God's Sovereignty Over Empires",
     "Author & Date: Daniel, a Jewish exile who rose to high position in the Babylonian and then Persian courts.",
     "Historical Setting: Spans the Babylonian exile through the early Persian period, with remarkably detailed, historically-verified knowledge of ancient Near Eastern court life and the succession of empires.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Exiled Jews needing courage to remain faithful under pagan pressure, and assurance that their God still reigned over even the mightiest empires.",
     "Purpose: To demonstrate God's sovereignty over every pagan empire and sustain hope in His ultimate, eternal kingdom."),
    ("Hosea", "God's Relentless, Wounded Love",
     "Author & Date: The prophet Hosea, ministering in the northern kingdom of Israel shortly before its fall to Assyria (8th century BC).",
     "Historical Setting: The northern kingdom's political instability and rampant idolatry (especially Baal worship) preceded its destruction in 722 BC.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Hosea's own painful marriage to an unfaithful wife becomes a living metaphor for Israel's spiritual adultery against God.",
     "Purpose: To portray God's relentless, wounded love for an unfaithful people and call them to repentance and return."),
    ("Joel", "The Day of the Lord",
     "Author & Date: The prophet Joel (date debated, possibly pre-exilic), using a devastating locust plague as an image of coming judgment.",
     "Historical Setting: An agrarian society for whom a locust plague meant genuine famine and economic collapse, used as a vivid picture of the coming 'day of the LORD.'",
     "Audience & Occasion: Judah, called to repentance and fasting in response to the immediate crisis.",
     "Purpose: To warn of coming judgment while promising future restoration and a Spirit-empowered renewal 'on all people' (later quoted by Peter at Pentecost, Acts 2)."),
    ("Amos", "Justice and True Worship",
     "Author & Date: A shepherd from Judah sent to prophesy against the northern kingdom of Israel during a time of prosperity and complacency (8th century BC).",
     "Historical Setting: Israel's prosperity under Jeroboam II masked deep social injustice -- exploitation of the poor coexisting with confident religious observance.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A comfortable, religiously active society that had disconnected worship from justice.",
     "Purpose: To insist that true worship is inseparable from justice and care for the vulnerable."),
    ("Obadiah", "Pride Precedes Judgment",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, the shortest Old Testament book, an oracle against Edom.",
     "Historical Setting: Edom, descended from Esau and thus related to Israel, gloated over and exploited Judah's disaster rather than helping a kinsman nation.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Judah, needing assurance that God holds even their bitter relatives accountable.",
     "Purpose: To affirm that God judges pride and betrayal, and promises eventual restoration for His people."),
    ("Jonah", "Grace for the Enemy",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, concerning the reluctant prophet Jonah, sent to preach repentance to Nineveh, capital of Israel's brutal enemy Assyria.",
     "Historical Setting: Assyria was widely feared for extreme brutality, making Jonah's reluctance to see them shown mercy understandable, if ultimately wrong.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Israelites tempted toward nationalistic resentment rather than compassion for even their worst enemies.",
     "Purpose: To reveal God's compassion extends even to Israel's fiercest enemies, and to expose the ugliness of resenting that grace."),
    ("Micah", "Justice, Mercy, Humility",
     "Author & Date: A contemporary of Isaiah, prophesying against both Israel and Judah's social injustice and empty ritual.",
     "Historical Setting: Like Amos, Micah confronts a society whose religious performance had become disconnected from ethical treatment of the vulnerable.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A nation needing to hear that hollow religious ritual could never substitute for genuine justice and humility.",
     "Purpose: To call for justice, mercy, and humility over empty religious performance (6:8), while pointing forward to a promised Ruler from Bethlehem (5:2)."),
    ("Nahum", "Nineveh's Second Warning",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, prophesying Nineveh's coming destruction (fulfilled in 612 BC).",
     "Historical Setting: A generation after Nineveh's repentance under Jonah's preaching, the city had returned to brutal violence and now faced final judgment.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Judah, needing reassurance that their brutal Assyrian oppressor would not escape justice forever.",
     "Purpose: To affirm that God's patience has limits, and persistent, unrepentant evil will eventually be judged."),
    ("Habakkuk", "Faith That Questions Honestly",
     "Author & Date: Unknown, a prophet wrestling honestly with God over why He would use the even more wicked Babylonians to judge Judah.",
     "Historical Setting: Written as Babylon's rise threatened Judah, raising hard questions about God's justice and timing.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Believers struggling with unresolved questions about God's ways in current events.",
     "Purpose: To model honest complaint to God that matures into settled trust, summarized in the declaration that 'the righteous person will live by his faithfulness.'"),
    ("Zephaniah", "Judgment and Joyful Restoration",
     "Author & Date: A prophet during King Josiah's reforms, warning of the coming 'day of the LORD.'",
     "Historical Setting: Judah's idolatry and complacency persisted even amid Josiah's reform efforts.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A nation needing urgent warning alongside genuine hope of a purified, joyful remnant.",
     "Purpose: To call for urgent repentance while promising restored joy and God's own delight over His people (3:17)."),
    ("Haggai", "Rebuild God's House First",
     "Author & Date: A short post-exilic prophet, ministering around 520 BC, roughly 18 years after the first return from exile.",
     "Historical Setting: Temple rebuilding had stalled amid opposition and self-interest, while the people had comfortably rebuilt their own houses instead.",
     "Audience & Occasion: The returned Jewish community, distracted from God's priorities by their own comfort.",
     "Purpose: To call God's priorities above personal comfort and promise greater glory for the second temple than the first."),
    ("Zechariah", "Encouragement Through Vision",
     "Author & Date: A post-exilic prophet, contemporary with Haggai, encouraging the Temple rebuilding through symbolic night visions and later oracles.",
     "Historical Setting: The same discouraged post-exilic community as Haggai's audience, needing both immediate encouragement and long-range hope.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A struggling, small community needing to see their present rebuilding within God's much larger future plan.",
     "Purpose: To encourage the discouraged post-exilic community while unveiling striking, detailed messianic prophecies fulfilled centuries later in Christ (the pierced one, the humble king on a donkey, thirty pieces of silver)."),
    ("Malachi", "Closing the Old Testament, Looking Forward",
     "Author & Date: The last Old Testament prophet, confronting the post-exilic community's spiritual apathy.",
     "Historical Setting: A community roughly a century removed from the return, grown spiritually complacent -- careless worship, unfaithful marriages, withheld tithes.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A people going through religious motions without genuine covenant devotion.",
     "Purpose: To call a spiritually complacent people back to genuine covenant faithfulness while closing the Old Testament looking forward to a coming messenger (fulfilled in John the Baptist) before 'the great and dreadful day of the LORD.'"),
]

